<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Korea and Japan</p><h1 class="st-title">Jinhan</h1><p class="st-meta">100 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A confederacy of twelve statelets in southeastern Korea, one of which, Saro, grew into the kingdom of Silla.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Jinhan formed in the 1st century BCE in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daegu">Daegu</a> area east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakdong-river">Nakdong</a> river. Chinese records claim its founders were refugees fleeing forced labour under the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/qin-dynasty">Qin dynasty</a>, which may reflect migration from the north after the fall of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gojoseon">Gojoseon</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Twelve statelets, each with chiefs of graded rank, farmed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, raised silkworms and rode horses and oxen. They produced iron like their <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/byeonhan">Byeonhan</a> neighbours and traded it widely. Early on they are said to have acknowledged the Jin king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/mahan">Mahan</a> as overlord, and their customs and language were close to Byeonhan's.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The statelet of Saro, centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gyeongju">Gyeongju</a>, gradually absorbed its neighbours from the 3rd century, and by the early 4th century Jinhan had given way to the kingdom of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/silla">Silla</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/jinhan.jpg" alt="Jinhan" width="320" height="501"><figcaption>Image: Asieon · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1st_century_Korea_Jinhan_highlighted.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>100 BCE – 300 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Indigenous beliefs</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/south-korea">South Korea</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Jinhan confederacy, Chinhan</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=100&amp;focus=jinhan" aria-label="Jinhan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7913 859 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Jinhan lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8260 1139h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8233 1115h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M8233 1115h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day South Korea. <a href="/atlas?year=100&amp;focus=jinhan">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
